-- title:
--   First-order action of level relabelling on Drinfeld origin parameters
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $q$ is a prime subject to the two numerical hypotheses `hq2 : q ≠ 2` and `hq : 5 ≤ q`, $\ell$ is a prime with `hℓ3 : 3 ≤ ℓ`, $M'$ is a nonzero natural number, and $A_0$ is a commutative ring.
--
--   *Stability of the level structures under variable change.* The hypothesis `hℓ` states that for every $A_0$-algebra $T$, every Weierstrass curve $W$ over $T$, every variable change $C$ and every `LevelPData` $D = (x_P,y_P,x_Q,y_Q)$ over $T$, if $D$ is a level-$\ell$ structure on $W$ (the two points satisfy the affine equation, $x_P$ and $x_Q$ are roots of $W.\mathrm{pre}\Psi_\ell$, and the two independence elements `indepElt W ℓ x_P x_Q`, `indepElt W ℓ x_Q x_P` are units) then the transformed data $D.\mathrm{variableChange}\,C$ is a level-$\ell$ structure on $C \bullet W$. The hypothesis `hM` states the analogous stability for `IsGamma0PowAt W p k h` (for $p^k = 2$ the two-kernel condition, otherwise the cyclic-generator-kernel condition on $h$) under $h \mapsto$ `kernelVariableChangeDeg C (gamma0PowDeg p k) h`.
--
--   *Group-law data and graded realisations.* $\mathcal G$ is a family `GroupLaws A₀` assigning to each $A_0$-algebra $T$, each projective Weierstrass curve $W$ over $T$ and each proof that $\Delta_W$ is a unit a relative group law on the projective model; `h𝒢` asserts that each of these laws is chord–tangent, in the sense that a points-evaluation `IsPointsEval` exists for it, and `h𝒢O` that the identity section of each of these laws admits an origin chart $\chi$ with $\chi(\mathrm{xOverY}) = 0$ and $\chi(\mathrm{zOverY}) = 0$. Further, $\mathcal T$ is a `LevelTransport A₀ 𝒢 q`, i.e. a functorial transport of raw Drinfeld pairs along $A_0$-algebra maps and along variable changes, compatible with identities and composition and preserving the level-$q$ condition; `h𝒯` is its section-transport property, which says that the transported sections, composed with the graded maps `Proj.map` coming from a variable-change homomorphism respectively a coefficient homomorphism, recover the original sections respectively their pullbacks along $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the coefficient map. The hypothesis `hℓA` says that $\ell$ is a unit in $A_0$; `hVC` and `hCO` assert, for every $A_0$-algebra $T$ (resp. every $A_0$-algebra map $f : T \to T'$), the existence of a graded ring homomorphism between the graded coordinate rings `projModelGradingCR` of the relevant projective models, carrying the irrelevant ideal of the target below the image of the irrelevant ideal of the source, and satisfying `IsVariableChangeHom` (resp. `IsCoefficientHom`), i.e. acting on the classes of constants and of the three coordinates by the explicit formulas of a variable change (resp. by the coefficient map on constants and the identity on coordinates).
--
--   *The moduli problem and its representing object.* Write $\mathcal D$ for the rigid Weierstrass datum `rigidDataPow A₀ ℓ M' q hℓ hM 𝒢 𝒯`: a raw point of $\mathcal D$ over an $A_0$-algebra $T$ consists of a Weierstrass curve over $T$ with unit discriminant together with level data in three parts — a family of kernel polynomials indexed by the prime factors $p$ of $M'$, each satisfying `IsGamma0PowAt` at $p^{v_p(M')}$; a `LevelPData` which is a level-$\ell$ structure; and a raw Drinfeld pair, namely a projective curve with two sections $P, Q$, subject to the condition that its curve is the given one and that $(P,Q)$ is a Drinfeld basis of level $q$ for the group law supplied by $\mathcal G$ — and the points $\mathcal D.\mathrm{Pt}\,T$ are the raw points modulo variable change. $P_0$ is a `LevelModuliPackageAbs` for the associated moduli datum: an $A_0$-algebra $B_0$ together with a point `univ` over $B_0$ such that for each $A_0$-algebra $T$ every point over $T$ is the image of `univ` under a unique $A_0$-algebra map $B_0 \to T$, that map being `P₀.classify`. Finally $x$ is a raw datum over $B_0$ whose class `Quot.mk _ x` is `P₀.univ` (hypothesis `hx`).
--
--   *The deformation ring.* $R$ is a noetherian local domain, complete for the adic topology of its maximal ideal and an $A_0$-algebra, and $\iota : B_0 \to R$ is an $A_0$-algebra map. $k$ is a field of characteristic $q$ in which $\ell$ and $M'$ are nonzero (`hℓk`, `hM'k`), and `resR : R → k` is surjective with kernel the maximal ideal of $R$. $W_0$ is a complete discrete valuation domain with maximal ideal generated by $q$ (`hW₀`) and surjective residue map `res₀ : W₀ → k` with kernel the maximal ideal; $R$ is a $W_0$-algebra, $W_0$ an $A_0$-algebra, the tower is compatible, and `hresR₀` says that `resR` restricted along $W_0 \to R$ is `res₀`. The hypothesis `hfac` is the universal-deformation property relative to $\iota$: for every artinian local $W_0$- and $A_0$-algebra $T$ with compatible tower, every surjection `resT : T → k` with kernel the maximal ideal which restricts to `res₀` on $W_0$, and every $A_0$-algebra map $\varphi : B_0 \to T$ whose residue agrees with that of $\iota$, there is a unique $W_0$-algebra map $\Phi : R \to T$ with `resT ∘ Φ = resR` and $\Phi \circ \iota = \varphi$.
--
--   *Formal groups, charts and the Drinfeld basis.* Put $X := \mathcal D.\mathrm{mapRing}\ \iota\ x$, a raw datum over $R$, and let $E$ be the curve of its Drinfeld-pair component `X.level.2.2`, with sections $P$ and $Q$. $F$ is a commutative formal group over $R$ whose power series is the fixed formal group law of $E$ (`hFW`), and $F_0$ a commutative formal group over $k$ whose power series is the fixed formal group law of the reduction of $E$ along `resR` (`hF₀W`); `hF₀` says that $F_0$ satisfies `IsDrinfeldBasisAdic ⊥ q 0 0`, i.e. relative to the zero ideal its $q$-th series is a unit power series times the Drinfeld divisor at $(0,0)$. The hypothesis `hssJ` says that for every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ of characteristic $q$ and every ring map $\iota_\Omega : k \to \Omega$, the image of $\mathrm{resR}(\iota(P_0.j_0))$ lies in [`ModularCurve.ssJSet q Ω`](def/ModularCurve_SupersingularModuli.html#L7), the set of $j \in \Omega$ such that every elliptic Weierstrass curve over $\Omega$ with that $j$-invariant has no nonzero $q$-torsion point. Next, $\chi_P$ and $\chi_Q$ are ring maps from the origin chart ring of $E$ to $R$ such that $\chi_P$ is an origin chart for $P$ and $\chi_Q$ one for $Q$, with `originParam` and `originW` of each lying in the maximal ideal (`hP`, `hQ`); `hBC` says that $F_0$ is the base change of $F$ along `resR`; `hDr` that $F$ satisfies `IsDrinfeldBasisAdic` for the maximal ideal of $R$, the level $q$ and the two origin parameters $x_0 :=$ `originParam χP`, $x_1 :=$ `originParam χQ`; and `hmax` that the maximal ideal of $R$ is generated by $x_0$ and $x_1$. The hypothesis `huniv` is the universal property of $R$ as a deformation ring with Drinfeld basis: for every artinian local $W_0$-algebra $T$ with surjection `resT : T → k` with kernel the maximal ideal restricting to `res₀`, every commutative formal group $G$ over $T$ which is the base change of $F_0$ along `resT`, and all $y_0, y_1$ in the maximal ideal of $T$ with $G$ satisfying `IsDrinfeldBasisAdic` for that ideal, $q$, $y_0$, $y_1$, there is a unique $W_0$-algebra map $\varphi : R \to T$ with `resT ∘ φ = resR` together with a formal group $F'$ over $T$ which is the base change of $F$ along $\varphi$ and a law isomorphism $\psi : F' \to G$ whose series has residue the identity series ($\mathrm{resT}$ of the $n$-th coefficient is $1$ for $n = 1$ and $0$ otherwise) and which sends $\varphi(x_0)$ to $y_0$ and $\varphi(x_1)$ to $y_1$ under `appAdic` for the maximal ideal of $T$.
--
--   *The relabelling data.* $\gamma$ is an element of $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb Z)$ lying in $\Gamma_0(M')$, and $\rho_\gamma$ is an automorphism of the moduli problem, i.e. a family of self-maps of the point sets commuting with base change and preserving the $j$-invariant. The hypothesis `hpin` pins $\rho_\gamma$ down on fields: for every field $T$ which is an $A_0$-algebra, all raw data $y, y'$ over $T$ and every proof that the discriminant of the curve of $y.\mathrm{level}.2.2$ is a unit, if $y'$ and $y$ have the same curve and the same $\Gamma_0$-component, if the level-$\ell$ component of $y'$ is the $\gamma$-relabelling `LevelRelabelling.LevelPData.relabel` of that of $y$ (form $\gamma_{00}P + \gamma_{10}Q$ and $\gamma_{01}P + \gamma_{11}Q$ in the affine point group and read off coordinates) and if the Drinfeld component of $y'$ is `LevelRelabelling.RawDrinfeldPair.relabel 𝒢 γ` applied to that of $y$ (replace $(P,Q)$ by the $\mathbb Z$-linear combinations with the columns of $\gamma$ for the group law $\mathcal G$), then $\rho_\gamma$ carries the class of $y$ to the class of $y'$. The hypothesis `hfix` says that $\iota$ composed with the classifying map of $\rho_\gamma(\mathrm{univ})$ agrees with $\iota$ modulo the maximal ideal of $R$, and $\theta_0 : R \to R$ is a $W_0$-algebra endomorphism with $\theta_0 \circ \iota = \iota \circ P_0.\mathrm{classify}(\rho_\gamma(\mathrm{univ}))$ (`hcompl`) which is the identity modulo the maximal ideal (`hres`).
--
--   *The comparison over $R$.* $V$ is a variable change over $R$, the discriminant of $E$ is a unit (`hΔ`), and $x'$ is a raw datum over $R$ obtained by applying $V$ to the transport of $X$ along $\theta_0$ (`hact`), subject to: $x'$ has the same underlying curve as $X$ (`hcurve`); the same $\Gamma_0$-component (`hlev1`); Drinfeld component equal to the $\gamma$-relabelling `LevelRelabelling.RawDrinfeldPair.relabel 𝒢 γ` of that of $X$ (`hlev22`); and level-$\ell$ component which, after mapping to the fraction field of $R$, equals the $\gamma$-relabelling `LevelRelabelling.LevelPData.relabel` of the level-$\ell$ component of $X$ over that fraction field (`hlev21`).
--
--   Under these hypotheses the conclusion is the conjunction of the two congruences modulo the square of the maximal ideal of $R$:
--   $$\theta_0(x_0) - (V.u)^{-1}\bigl(\gamma_{00}\,x_0 + \gamma_{10}\,x_1\bigr) \in \mathfrak m_R^2, \qquad \theta_0(x_1) - (V.u)^{-1}\bigl(\gamma_{01}\,x_0 + \gamma_{11}\,x_1\bigr) \in \mathfrak m_R^2,$$
--   where $x_0 =$ `originParam χP`, $x_1 =$ `originParam χQ`, $(V.u)^{-1}$ denotes the image in $R$ of the inverse of the unit $u$ of the variable change $V$, and the matrix entries of $\gamma$ are taken as integers mapped into $R$.
--
--   This is the first-order computation underlying the functoriality of level relabelling on a rigidified universal deformation ring at a supersingular point: it identifies the linear part of the relabelling endomorphism $\theta_0$ on the Drinfeld coordinates $(x_0,x_1)$ as multiplication by $u(V)^{-1}$ composed with the matrix $\gamma$ acting by columns. It is used by [`ModularCurve.LevelModuliPackageAbs.exists_originParam_linearPart_of_algHom_comp_eq_classify_act_of_problemAut_relabel_gamma0Pow_of_mem_ssJSet`](thm.html#ModularCurve.LevelModuliPackageAbs.exists_originParam_linearPart_of_algHom_comp_eq_classify_act_of_problemAut_relabel_gamma0Pow_of_mem_ssJSet), which extracts the linear part of the $\Gamma_0(M')$-action on the supersingular deformation ring.
